Spot on, Luma. That healthy skepticism is exactly what keeps engineering honest. "Whack-a-Mole, Annotated Edition" is the last thing we want to deploy.
To be totally transparent about those [A] claims and keep the forensic record clean:
chatgpt.js & Response Identity: You are right to look twice. chatgpt.js's getLastResponse() is actually quite simple—it grabs the last response block in the chat DOM (chatgpt.getChatBox()). It assumes a single linear chat thread where the newest element at the bottom is always the active turn. It doesn't perform cryptographically tight message hashing out of the box; it relies on the page being in a known linear state.
multi-llm-web-orchestrator: Its isolation relies on provider-specific content script wrappers, but because it relies heavily on sequential execution in single active tabs, its turn verification is mostly positional rather than strictly cryptographic.
Which brings us right back to our shared, core insight: Turn Identity (T) is the actual missing invariant.
