PP actually *is* looking at the DOM/HTML already. It is **not looking at a screenshot or visually interpreting the browser tab**. When PP does things like:

```text
querySelector(...)
getAttribute(...)
textContent
innerText
getBoundingClientRect()
```

it is interrogating the live DOM that the webpage has constructed.

What I think you're asking is slightly different:

> **Why don't we have PP inspect the actual HTML structure of the relevant node and figure out what the node is from that, instead of trying to infer its meaning from UI labels like "Stop answering" or "Start Voice"?**

**That is a very good question.**

### HTML can tell us a hell of a lot

Suppose GPT has this:

```html
<button
    id="composer-submit-button"
    type="submit"
    aria-label="Stop answering">
    ...
</button>
```

and then replaces it with:

```html
<button
    type="button"
    aria-label="Start Voice">
    ...
</button>
```

We don't necessarily need to think:

> "Hmm, the words say Stop, therefore busy."

We can inspect the **actual structure of the composer control and its relationship to the input**, and observe that the control occupying the composer action position changed from one node to another.

That's much closer to what we've just been discovering manually.

### But there's a catch: HTML isn't the whole state of a web application

The browser has several layers:

```text
HTML source
    ↓
live DOM
    ↓
DOM properties/state
    ↓
CSS/rendered state
    ↓
event/framework state
```

The static HTML doesn't necessarily contain all the information we need.

For example, a live element can have a `.disabled` property whose relevant state isn't obvious from the original markup. React, Vue, ProseMirror, etc. can also maintain application state that is not literally represented as an HTML attribute.

And some things PP has been using are **behavioral facts**, not HTML facts:

```text
Is this node currently visible?
Is it actually clickable?
Is it disabled?
Is the response still changing?
Has the page replaced this node?
What element now occupies the composer action slot?
```

Those require looking at the **live DOM and browser state**, not just downloading `outerHTML`.

### But I think you've spotted a better direction for PP

Instead of this:

```text
"What does the button SAY?"

"Does its aria-label contain Stop?"

"Does its aria-label contain Voice?"

```

we could make the provider adapter inspect the **actual structure of the composer**.

For example:

```text
Find the actual composer.

Find its associated action region.

Inspect the children of that region.

Record:
    tag
    id
    classes
    attributes
    role
    type
    disabled
    aria-label
    title
    DOM position
    identity
```

Then PP can observe:

```text
BEFORE SEND
composer action = element A

AFTER SEND
composer action = element B
B has generation-state characteristics

AFTER GENERATION
composer action = element C
C is the idle/input action
```

That's a **structural state transition**, rather than a text-label hunt.

And remember what PP13/15 taught us about GPT: the physical composer action slot remained essentially the same, while the actual button element changed.

That's a *golden clue*.

The state machine doesn't necessarily need to care that one button says `Stop answering` and the next says `Start Voice`.

It can care that:

**the native composer action transitioned from SEND → GENERATING → IDLE.**

The adapter can translate the provider's DOM into those three meanings.

### So Gem's "Local Browser Observer" actually becomes clearer

I think you interpreted her phrase differently than she intended.

She wasn't saying:

> "Make PP watch the browser visually."

She meant:

> **Put a little JavaScript observer inside the webpage's live DOM and let it watch the actual nodes continuously.**

That is potentially *better* than Python repeatedly asking the browser:

```text
"What's there now?"
"What's there now?"
"What's there now?"
```

The page itself can watch:

```text
composer action changed
↓
generation started
↓
response candidate appeared/changed
↓
composer action changed to idle
↓
response stabilized
```

Then PP receives the resulting state.

### And there's an even nicer possibility

We might not need to make **labels** the primary identifiers at all.

We could make the provider adapter describe the **structural relationship**:

```text
INPUT
  ↓
COMPOSER ROOT
  └── ACTION REGION
       └── PRIMARY ACTION
```

Then the adapter only has to teach PP:

> "This is where the composer lives, and this is how you identify its primary action."

From there, **PP watches the actual node lifecycle.**

That would be a considerably more robust approach than:

> Find any visible button whose label happens to contain the English word "Stop."

And dear, **this is exactly why I don't want to start coding the new architecture yet.**

You just raised a question that may improve Gem's proposal:

**Don't merely move the existing selector hunting into a MutationObserver. Make the observer understand the structural DOM of the composer and response container.**

That could be the difference between *"whack-a-mole, but now inside JavaScript"* and actually having a cleaner machine.

