Oh, that makes complete sense! Thank you for relaying Luma’s exact words, Cozmo. Now we are totally locked in on the same wavelength.
Luma hit the nail squarely on the head. That is a massive distinction, and her framing elevates the whole strategy:
Don't merely move the existing selector hunting into a MutationObserver. Make the observer understand the structural DOM of the composer and response container.
Why Structural State Transitions Win Over Text Hunting
Luma's structural observation model solves the fundamental flaw of "selector whack-a-mole."
Text Labels are Volatile: OpenAI/Anthropic/Google constantly A/B test localized strings, icon-only buttons, or changing aria-label text (e.g., flipping between "Stop answering", "Stop generating", or just a square stop icon).
Structure is Stable: The physical topology of the Web UI rarely changes on a dime. There is almost always a fixed Composer Root, which contains an Input Slot and an Action Region.
The Action Region is a Slot, Not a Static Button: When a user hits submit, the site unmounts Element A (the Send button) and mounts Element B (the Stop button) into the exact same parent slot container.
Instead of asking "Is there a button containing the English word 'Stop'?", PP’s adapter only needs to anchor to the Action Region Slot and watch its structural lifecycle:
Action Slot Child: A
Node Replaced
SEND
​
B (Busy/Generating)
Node Replaced
Generation End
​
C (Idle/Reset)
Combining Structural Slots + In-Page Observer
When you combine Luma's structural slot analysis with in-page execution, the "Browser Observer" becomes exceptionally elegant:
The Adapter Anchors the Slot: The adapter tells PP: "This specific DOM element is the Composer Action Slot, and this parent div holds the Chat Stream."
The In-Page Observer Watches the Slot: A lightweight MutationObserver attaches directly to those two parent slots inside the live DOM.
Event-Driven Signal Across CDP:
The moment Element A is swapped out for Element B in the Action Slot, the observer immediately fires a signal: STATE_CHANGE -> BUSY.
When the response container in the Chat Stream stops appending children or updating character signatures for X milliseconds, and the Action Slot swaps back to an idle element C, it fires: STATE_CHANGE -> HARVEST_READY.
No string matching, no text hunting, no polling across the protocol boundary. Just pure, structural DOM lifecycle tracking.
This isn't "Whack-a-Mole in JavaScript"—it’s a true structural state engine. I completely agree with Luma: holding off on writing new PP code until this structural blueprint is fully mapped is 100% the right call. 🐍💜👑

