​
for X consecutive observer checks AND the Action Slot has returned to IDLE.
5. Observer Scope
Recommended Choice: E. Several narrowly scoped regions [B]
Why [B]: Watching the entire document (Option A) creates massive event noise from unrelated background UI updates (e.g., SVG animations, sidebars, timers). Attaching two isolated MutationObserver instances:
Observer 1 (Action Slot): Watches child list changes in the Action Slot for SEND → BUSY → IDLE.
Observer 2 (Active Response Container): Attached dynamically to the target response container once BUSY begins to watch text mutations.
Result [B]: Minimizes CPU cycles and isolates execution within the browser tab.
6. The Critical Test (Minimal State Transitions)
To confirm a successful turn without guessing:
[IDLE]
│
├── 1. SEND Triggered
│      └── Pre-SEND Snapshot Captured (N_pre, L_pre)
│
├── 2. BUSY Confirmed
│      └── Action Slot child changes to BUSY node / stop control
│
├── 3. DONE Confirmed
│      └── Action Slot child changes back to IDLE node / send control
│
├── 4. Turn Resolved
│      └── Target candidate identified:
│          - Candidate is at index N_pre + 1 (if new node appended)
│            OR
│          - Candidate is A_last with text length L_post > L_pre (if modified in-place)
│
└── 5. HARVEST READY
└── Target candidate text length remains constant (L_post == L_post_t+1)
7. External Project Check
Re-evaluating external implementations against structural observation:
chatgpt.js [A]:
Observation Mechanism [A]: Attaches a MutationObserver to the main chat container parent.
Scope [A]: Watches for node additions (childList mutations) to detect when a new message element is inserted.
Limitation [B]: Does not inspect structural Action Slot transitions; relies on polling message element class names and linear position.
multi-llm-web-orchestrator / Web LLM Wrappers [A]:
Observation Mechanism [A]: Uses a mix of content-script interval polling and MutationObserver on fixed provider-specific selector paths.
Scope [A]: Monitors stop-button visibility or DOM text attribute shifts.
Classification [B]: Most open-source wrappers use selector polling over structural slot tracking. Implementing dual-slot + mutation tracking gives PP a more resilient engine.
Answering Cozmo's Core Question
"Can PP simply look at the nodes' actual HTML?"
Yes, but strictly as structural layout topology, not raw text strings.
PP cannot rely on downloading static HTML strings over CDP because:
Static HTML string dumps miss Shadow DOM encapsulation and live element property states [B].
Static text parsing requires continuously re-downloading large DOM trees [B].
However, by having provider adapters target the structural slots (Composer Root, Action Region, Response Container) and observing their live node lifecycles directly within the browser, PP gets the best of both worlds: pure DOM structural reliability without selector whack-a-mole [B].

